# Scope

User clarified affected mode is Codex app-server + remote TUI, not Electron.
Required behavior is Leave paused after unattended restart, preserving goal
identity, objective, paused status and updatedAt. Screenshot not interpreted by
current provider; exact incident root cause unverified.

# Source findings

Existing responder requires current rendered state; Enter only when Leave paused
is selected. Windows ConPTY has a screen adapter; POSIX and direct paths lack
that adapter. Whole-screen quiet timer can be starved by unrelated redraws.
RPC channel injector opens before TUI and has no shared modal-readiness gate.
Remote relaunch mutates process env; the next TUI receives an existing env mapping,
so flag propagation needs an exact regression rather than an assumed success.

# Verification in progress

Owned native baseline/candidate harness and focused production regression work
are delegated. No successful repair is claimed yet. Live sessions are untouched.

# Native baseline observation

Installed 4907ba8 snapshot with real Codex 0.158.0 passed the seeded paused-goal
remote TUI restart scenario (run-codex-remote-baseline-paused-111656). The existing
handler logged seen/select/dismissed, same thread resumed, goal objective/status
paused/updatedAt remained unchanged, post-restart external input replied, and
owned listeners were absent after cleanup. Thus this is NOT a reproduction of
the user's reported failure, and candidate changes cannot claim to fix that
incident based on this baseline. Screenshots were unavailable (0x0 window);
rendered terminal text, RPC readback and lifecycle logs were captured locally.

# Regression red phase

During parallel implementation the added exact-file regression selection ran
122 tests with 3 failures and 1 error against not-yet-updated production:
- repeated unrelated viewport redraw postpones Leave paused selection;
- remote restart child env lacks CIEL_RUNTIME_STARTUP_AUTO_ANSWER in the unit
  session wrapper without outer dispatch's environment normalization;
- injector accepts direct submit before any terminal readiness gate;
- rendered_input_ready is a new planned method absent from baseline.
These are targeted red regressions, not the actual reported incident reproduction.

# Candidate execution evidence

Candidate run-codex-remote-candidate-sequential-113226 passed paused-goal
acceptance. Same goal thread/objective/status=paused/updatedAt were unchanged.
Timeline: prompt seen 11:33:06, Leave paused select 11:33:07, dismissed 11:33:08,
external message injection 11:33:15. Owned listeners absent after teardown.
Targeted selection 454 tests passed, 42 skipped; ruff/compile passed.

Earlier candidate-gated-112924 exited 3221225786 before router health/scenario
with AttachConsole failure; it is a failed attempt, not fix evidence. Concurrent
native-console tests were running, but causality of that exit is not established.
Sequential rerun passed. Baseline success remains a limit on incident attribution.

# Review corrections

- Relaunch env copying now happens per-attempt from the original mutable mapping,
  after the outer relaunch callback can renew credentials; this prevents stale
  credentials on second/subsequent restart without mutating the caller for the
  auto-answer flag.
- The new RPC screen gate is Windows-only. POSIX is deliberately not closed by a
  new gate with no observer; existing terminal fail-closed limitations remain.
- Readiness stability now uses semantic ready/modal markers, not unrelated
  surrounding viewport changes.
- Targeted reviewed suite: 457 tests, zero failures/errors, 42 skips. No local
  whole-group sweep performed. Native reviewed rerun passed (reviewed-candidate-114817), with paused goal
objective/status/updatedAt unchanged, new input replied, owned listeners absent.

# Shipping scope

The change strengthens Windows app-server + remote TUI startup only. It does not
claim a repaired POSIX renderer, Electron UI automation, or a root cause for the
reported screenshot instance. Publication/local installation awaits exact-SHA
workflow verification.

